<h2 id="why-the-texas-license-matters">Why the Texas all-lines license is the one most adjusters hold</h2>
<p>The Texas Department of Insurance (TDI) issues an all-lines adjuster license that covers property, casualty, auto and workers comp claims. Adjusters in dozens of states hold it, even if they have never lived in Texas. Three reasons:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Designated home state (DHS).</strong> If your home state does not license adjusters, you can designate Texas as your home state and hold its resident-equivalent license.</li>
<li><strong>Broad reciprocity.</strong> Many states recognize the Texas license, so adding non-resident licenses elsewhere is usually a paperwork step instead of another exam.</li>
<li><strong>Local demand.</strong> Texas is one of the largest adjuster markets in the country, with hail, Gulf hurricanes, tornadoes and freezes generating claims every year.</li>
</ul>
<p>For the full state picture, read our <a href="/insurance-adjuster-advice/insurance-adjuster-salary-texas/">insurance adjuster salary in Texas</a> guide.</p>

