<h2 id="adding-more-licenses">Adding Florida and other licenses later</h2>
<p>Once you hold the Texas license, the next step for most storm-focused adjusters is the Florida 6-20 all-lines license, since Florida is the busiest hurricane market. Florida also issues emergency licenses after declared disasters, but a permanent license keeps you on deployment rosters. Louisiana, Georgia and the Carolinas are common additions after that. Each extra license widens the pool of storms you can work and smooths out income between Texas seasons.</p>

<h2 id="texas-public-adjuster-license">The Texas public adjuster license is separate</h2>
<p>Public adjusters represent policyholders rather than insurers, and Texas requires a separate public adjuster license for that work. Texas caps public adjuster fees at 10% of the settlement, so a $60,000 claim yields up to $6,000. Public adjusters commonly earn $50,000 to $100,000 a year, and established firms with commercial flow can exceed $150,000. Many start with the all-lines license, build claims experience, then add the public adjuster license.</p>
